  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nWeak warm front/wind shift will continue to work its way eastward \nacross the CWA into the evening hours, shifting winds to the west-\nnorthwest while it moves through. A cold front then drops southward \nthrough the region late tonight into Sunday morning while a surface \nridge axis also begins building in. Winds go rather light/variable \ncloser to 12Z Sunday, and could see a drop in temps around that time \nin favored cold spots like the James valley. Dropped lows a few \ndegrees from NBM in those areas. With the passage of the cold front \nlate tonight, slightly cooler high temps are forecast on Sunday, but \nreadings will still be above normal for this time of year. \n\nAnother push of mild air into the Northern Plains late Sunday \nthrough Sunday night as 850mb temps rise to around +13 to +16C over \nthe CWA. Some of this warmer air lingers around on Monday but temps \naloft will be cooling as we have another cold front passage. \nInherited NBM highs feature upper 50s to low 60s across the region \non Monday. \n\nModels showing a surface ridge axis once again moving overhead \nMonday night and lowered Min T's just a couple degrees in the James \nvalley.\n\nThe overall theme through the 7-day forecast period is mostly dry \nconditions and above normal temperatures. That said, models do hint \nat some colder air trying to move southward over the Northern Plains \nlater this upcoming week. Grand Ensemble showing 850mb temps \ngenerally from 0C to +5C across the CWA by 18Z Thursday. Forecast \nhighs later in the week do come down more into the 40s and 50s by \nthen, but still near to above normal for this time of year. \nIndications from models suggest this is only brief though as we warm \nback up heading into next weekend.",
